# Configuration for the two /api routes and the contact form.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local use, and set the same names in Vercel under Project
# Settings -> Environment Variables for deployments. Tick Production, Preview
# and Development — a name missing from Preview makes preview deploys fail in
# ways that look like code faults.
#
# The VITE_ prefix decides whether a value reaches the browser, and it cuts
# both ways in this file. Vite inlines VITE_* into the client bundle; anything
# without the prefix stays on the server. Read the note on each name before
# renaming it — one of them needs the prefix and the rest must never have it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- server side

# Origin of the Tripket API, no trailing path. Serves both routes: the
# schedules proxy reads /api/legs/featured-schedules and the inquiry proxy
# posts to /api/inquiries, each with the path fixed in its handler so no part
# of the URL is caller-controlled.
#
# Must be https for any public host; the proxies refuse plaintext http unless
# the host is localhost, 127.0.0.1, *.test or *.local, so a dev box works.
TRIPKET_API_BASE=

# Optional. Sent to the upstream as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Both
# endpoints appear to be public, so this is unset — the handlers only add the
# header when it exists. If the inquiry endpoint turns out to need a
# credential it answers 401, and the log line for that names this variable.
#
# This is a secret: it stays on the server and is never returned to the
# browser. Do not give it a VITE_ prefix.
# TRIPKET_API_TOKEN=

# Deliberately unset. The inquiry API accepts `g-recaptcha-response` in the
# body, which means it holds the reCAPTCHA secret and verifies the token
# itself, so this project only forwards the token.
#
# Setting this switches local verification on — and if the upstream is still
# verifying too, *every submission fails*. A reCAPTCHA token is single use:
# our check would consume it and the upstream would then reject a spent
# token, presenting as a validation error on a form with nothing wrong in it.
# See api/_lib/recaptcha.js before you set this.
#
# The secret belongs wherever siteverify is called. Today that is the
# developer's server, not here.
# RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY=

# Only read when RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY is set. Google's default is 0.5.
# RECAPTCHA_MIN_SCORE=0.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- client side

# The reCAPTCHA v3 *site* key. This one is public by design — every reCAPTCHA
# site ships it in its markup — so the VITE_ prefix is correct here and only
# here. What stops someone else using it is the domain list in the reCAPTCHA
# admin console plus the server-side verification, not secrecy.
#
# Two things to know:
#   - Vite inlines this at build time, so changing it needs a redeploy, not
#     just a restart.
#   - it must be a v3 key. A v2 key looks identical and fails at execute().
#
# Also add your domains in the reCAPTCHA admin console — tripketph.com,
# www.tripketph.com, localhost, and your *.vercel.app preview host. A missing
# entry there is the usual cause of a key that works in one place only.
#
# Without this the contact form falls back to composing an email, and logs a
# warning to the console saying so.
VITE_RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=
